## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a compact computer vision pipeline with practical on-device adaptation under tight latency constraints. It also probes understanding of data strategy and evaluation beyond single-object pose tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight detection with 2D bounding boxes
- Depth proxy estimation from object size
- Simple tracking with temporal consistency
- On-device few-shot adaptation (3-shot)
- Counting metrics and evaluation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: detection + tracking update loop
for (const frame of video) {
  const detections = detector.predict(frame);
  const depthProxy = estimateDepth(detections, calibration);
  const tracked = tracker.update(detections, depthProxy);
  yield { boxes: tracked, count: countPackages(tracked) };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes on-device real-time hazard detection and 3D distance estimation under unseen categories, reflecting safety-critical constraints common in field deployments.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time monocular depth on edge
- On-device meta-learning (5-shot)
- Synthetic+real data with domain randomization
- Temporal tracking and occlusion robustness
- Evaluation: detection, depth error, latency

## Code Example

```javascript
function forward(img, backbone, detectorHead, depthHead) {
  const feats = backbone.extract(img);
  const boxes = detectorHead.predict(feats);
  const depth = depthHead.predict(feats);
  return { boxes, depth };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you ablate depth head accuracy vs. detector latency?
- What failure modes dominate in cluttered sites, and how would you mitigate them?

## Why This Is Asked
This assesses end-to-end CV system design on monocular input, 3D reconstruction, semantic/kinematic outputs, and on-device adaptation, under real-world constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular depth/3D reconstruction
- Voxel-based 3D representation and BEV outputs
- Multi-task objectives: occupancy, semantics, motion tracking
- Self-supervision with ego-motion and photometric consistency
- Lightweight 3-shot on-device adaptation via adapters

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation sketch
async function runPipeline(frame) {
  const latent = encode(frame)
  const voxels = decodeToVoxels(latent)
  return { occupancy: voxelToOccupancy(voxels), beings: trackPedestrians(latent) }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evaluate 3D occupancy accuracy vs. GT? 
- How would you extend to multi-camera fusion or hinge on motion blur?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-world constraints: on-device adaptation, occlusion robustness, and AR-friendly latency for indoor property inspections. Probes data strategy and robust evaluation.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular 6-DoF pose from RGB
- On-device 5-shot adaptation
- Synthetic + real data strategy
- Occlusion-robust tracking
- Edge GPU latency targets

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: simple adaptation step
function adapt(batch) {
  for (const [x, y] of batch) {
    const pred = model.predict(x);
    const loss = poseLoss(pred, y);
    optimizer.step(loss);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you compare on-device adaptation strategies under varying illumination?
- What metrics best detect drift in furniture appearance over time?

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates robust multi-hand 3D pose with fine-grained articulation under occlusion, plus on-device adaptation for real-world diversity.

## Key Concepts
- 3D hand pose (finger joints), hand mesh (MANO)
- Temporal tracking and occlusion handling
- On-device few-shot adaptation and edge latency

## Code Example
```javascript
function totalLoss(key3d, pred3d, mesh3d, predMesh3d, tio, contactPred, contactGT){
  const lKey = mse(key3d, pred3d);
  const lMesh = l2(mesh3d, predMesh3d);
  const lTemp = mse(pred3d, tio);
  const lContact = crossEntropy(contactGT, contactPred);
  return 0.4*lKey + 0.3*lMesh + 0.2*lTemp + 0.1*lContact;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evaluate hand-object contact reliability in clutter?
- What ablations would you run to isolate the impact of articulation accuracy vs. contact estimation?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design cross-modal, real-time computer vision pipelines that integrate on-device adaptation with differentiable geometry for robotic manipulation and planning in unstructured environments.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time monocular depth and surface normals estimation from a single RGB camera
- Differentiable probabilistic mesh prediction integrated with motion planning
- Few-shot (5-shot) on-device adaptation using synthetic pre-training followed by real-world fine-tuning
- Multi-task learning with shared encoders and task-specific heads

## Code Example

```javascript
// Core inference pipeline sketch
function forwardPass(frame) {
  const features = sharedEncoder(frame);
  const depth = depthHead(features);
  const normals = normalHead(features);
  const mesh = meshHead(features);
  return { depth, normals, mesh };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you quantify uncertainty in the mesh predictions to ensure safe manipulation?
- What strategies would you use to handle reflective or texture-less surfaces where depth estimation typically fails?
- How does the system handle dynamic scene changes between adaptation and deployment?
